(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-cardiac-biomarkers-lipids-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiac Biomarkers & Lipids Quiz 2 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 46-year-old has total cholesterol 200 mg/dL, HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) 50 mg/dL and triglycerides 100 mg/dL, well below the 400 mg/dL limit for the Friedewald equation. What is the Friedewald LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol)?","choices":["150 mg/dL","50 mg/dL","130 mg/dL","170 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Friedewald: total cholesterol minus HDL-C minus triglycerides divided by 5, so 200 minus 50 minus 20 is 130 mg/dL. It is an estimate, not a measurement. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 59-year-old's troponin is being interpreted. Which troponin subunit has cardiac specificity?","choices":["Troponin C, the calcium-binding component","Troponin I, the inhibitory component","Troponin T, found only in cardiac muscle","None of the three subunits is cardiac"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Troponin I has cardiac specificity. Troponin T is primarily cardiac, but trace amounts are detected in skeletal muscle. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 62-year-old on lipid-lowering therapy is followed with repeated apoB (apolipoprotein B) levels. Why is apoB suited to that role?","choices":["It is genetic and fixed over life","It is modifiable and responds to treatment","It is a marker of acute inflammation","It measures HDL particles only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ApoB is a modifiable, treatment-responsive marker used repeatedly to guide and monitor lipid-lowering therapy, unlike lipoprotein(a), which is fixed and measured once. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 37-year-old with no symptoms asks whether she needs a lipoprotein(a) level. What does the current guidance say?","choices":["Measure it at least once in all adults","Measure it only after a heart attack","Measure it every year from age 40","Never measure it; it is untreatable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Current multisociety guidance recommends measuring lipoprotein(a) at least once in all adults for atherosclerotic risk assessment. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 58-year-old with diabetes has an LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) already below 70 mg/dL on therapy. Which test helps find remaining risk?","choices":["HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol)","Creatine kinase","Troponin","ApoB (apolipoprotein B)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ApoB is used when triglycerides are 150 mg/dL or above, in diabetes, or when LDL-C is below 70 mg/dL, to find residual risk carried by atherogenic particles. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 58-year-old still has an elevated troponin four weeks after a myocardial infarction. What does a late elevation relate to?","choices":["Higher left ventricular ejection fraction","A falsely positive assay","Lower left ventricular ejection fraction","A reduced risk of reinfarction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elevated late levels at 4 weeks are inversely related to left ventricular ejection fraction, which is how troponin estimates infarct size and impact. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 45-year-old with a high HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) asks what protein defines that particle. Which is it?","choices":["ApoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100)","ApoB-48 (apolipoprotein B-48)","Apo(a) (apolipoprotein(a))","ApoA-I (apolipoprotein A-I)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ApoA-I is the major structural protein of HDL, synthesized by the liver and intestines and found on all HDL particles. ApoA-II is second, on about two-thirds of them. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 49-year-old asks which lipoprotein is the principal driver of atherosclerotic disease. Which is it?","choices":["HDL (high-density lipoprotein)","LDL (low-density lipoprotein)","Chylomicrons","Albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. LDL is the major cholesterol carrier and the primary target for prevention; LDL cholesterol and cardiovascular risk have a log-linear relationship. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 50-year-old's panel shows total cholesterol 210 mg/dL and HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) 50 mg/dL. What is the non-HDL-C, the value followed against a risk-based goal?","choices":["260 mg/dL","160 mg/dL","110 mg/dL","210 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Non-HDL-C is total cholesterol minus HDL-C: 210 minus 50 is 160 mg/dL. It needs no triglyceride value and costs nothing extra. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 10-year-old with no family history comes for a well-child visit. Is a lipid screen indicated?","choices":["No, screening starts at age 19","No, children are never screened","Only if she is already obese","Yes, at ages 9 to 11"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Childhood screening at ages 9 to 11 identifies familial hypercholesterolemia and other lipid disorders; lipids are screened again at 19. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 35-year-old has lipoprotein(a) measured for the first time. How often does it generally need measuring?","choices":["Every year","Once in a lifetime","Every 5 years","After every statin change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is more than 90% genetically determined and stable over life, and needs no fasting, so a single measurement generally suffices. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 45-year-old has a C-reactive protein checked during an acute infection and again after recovery. How does the level behave?","choices":["It rises slowly and stays high for years","It rises quickly and drops rapidly","It stays constant throughout life","It falls during active infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. C-reactive protein is an acute-phase reactant from the liver: it rises quickly in response to inflammation and drops rapidly once the process resolves. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 63-year-old arrives with chest pain and a high-sensitivity troponin is drawn at presentation. When is the repeat drawn?","choices":["At 12 to 24 hours","At 1 to 2 hours","At 4 weeks","Never, one value is enough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serial testing at hour 0 and 1 to 2 hours is possible because the assay detects lower concentrations, which is what gives the faster rule-in or rule-out. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 56-year-old has just started a statin. When should the lipid panel be rechecked?","choices":["In 4 to 12 weeks","In 5 years","In 1 week","Only if he has symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Check lipids 4 to 12 weeks after starting or changing the dose of lipid-lowering therapy, then every 6 to 12 months. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 47-year-old's panel shows triglycerides of 480 mg/dL (desirable below 150 mg/dL). What is true of a Friedewald LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) on this sample?","choices":["It cannot be used","It is the most accurate estimate","It is falsely high but usable","It becomes a direct measurement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Friedewald equation cannot be used when triglycerides are 400 mg/dL or above. The Martin/Hopkins and Sampson/NIH (National Institutes of Health) equations are now preferred. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 69-year-old arrives 14 hours after chest pain began, near the troponin peak. Why may two serial values barely differ?","choices":["Values near the peak may not change","Troponin clears within a few hours","Troponin never rises in late presenters","The assay is invalid after 12 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With late presentation, values are above the 99th percentile and may sit near peak, so the delta between draws can be small. Early presenters show values rising from below the limit. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 54-year-old's 10-year PREVENT (Predicting Risk of cardiovascular disease EVENTs) estimate is 4%, in the borderline tier of 3% to under 5%. Besides discussing the estimate, what does the framework add for her?","choices":["Immediate cardiac catheterization","Serial troponin measurement","Stopping all lipid testing","Evaluating risk enhancers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At borderline risk the framework pairs the risk discussion with an evaluation of risk enhancers, such as an elevated lipoprotein(a) or triglycerides of 150 mg/dL or above. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 40-year-old with no risk factors is due a routine lipid profile and has eaten breakfast. Is the sample acceptable?","choices":["No, every lipid profile must be fasting","No, lipids can only be drawn at night","Yes, nonfasting is acceptable for most","Yes, but only lipoprotein(a) is valid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nonfasting is acceptable for most people. Fasting is reserved for triglycerides of 400 mg/dL or above, triglyceride disorders, or a family history of premature disease or genetic dyslipidemia. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman's screening panel shows total cholesterol 178 mg/dL (desirable below 200), triglycerides 90 mg/dL (desirable below 150) and HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) 62 mg/dL (higher is favorable). How is it interpreted?","choices":["Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Low HDL-C needing treatment","Suspected familial hypercholesterolemia","A favorable profile"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Total cholesterol and triglycerides are below their desirable limits and HDL-C is high, which is generally favorable. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 57-year-old has an apoB (apolipoprotein B) level measured. Which particles carry apoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100)?","choices":["Chylomicrons and HDL only","HDL, VLDL and chylomicrons","VLDL, IDL, LDL and lipoprotein(a)","Lipoprotein(a) and HDL only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ApoB-100 is synthesized by the liver and found on very low-density, intermediate-density and low-density lipoprotein and on lipoprotein(a), one per particle. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 58-year-old is evaluated for exertional chest pain rated 10 out of 10. Which question are cardiac biomarkers ordered to answer?","choices":["Is there acute cardiac injury or stress?","What is the long-term atherosclerotic risk?","How severe is any coronary stenosis?","Which coronary artery is occluded?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Biomarkers are the acute question. The lipid panel answers a different one, the patient's long-term atherosclerotic risk, which is why it belongs to the routine physical rather than the chest-pain workup. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has worsening dyspnea and leg edema. What does a raised B-type natriuretic peptide in her blood reflect?","choices":["Myocyte necrosis","Systemic inflammation","Skeletal muscle breakdown","Myocardial wall stretch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. B-type natriuretic peptide is released by the ventricles with volume or pressure overload. It reflects stretch rather than injury, a different mechanism from troponin, so the two are complementary. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 48-year-old has triglycerides of 900 mg/dL (desirable below 150 mg/dL). Which acute risk does this level carry?","choices":["Rhabdomyolysis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Pancreatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides of 500 mg/dL or above are severe hypertriglyceridemia with pancreatitis risk, and 1000 mg/dL or above is extreme. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 66-year-old with suspected myocardial infarction has a raised CK-MB (creatine kinase muscle-brain isoenzyme). Why is that result of little diagnostic use today?","choices":["Its cardiac specificity is low","It rises only after a week","It measures wall stretch only","It is falsely low in obesity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Creatine kinase is a cytosolic enzyme of skeletal and cardiac muscle, and even the MB fraction adds no diagnostic value for acute infarction once troponin is available. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 38-year-old has a markedly elevated C-reactive protein, far above the healthy range of less than 0.3 mg/dL. Which process typically produces the highest levels?","choices":["Uncomplicated obesity","Cigarette smoking","Acute bacterial infection","Normal pregnancy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Overall, the highest levels occur with bacterial infections; severe elevation above 50 mg/dL is generally seen in acute bacterial infection. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman had a borderline lipoprotein(a) before menopause. When is repeating it reasonable?","choices":["After menopause","After starting a statin","After fasting overnight","After a viral illness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Although a single lifetime measurement generally suffices, repeat measurement is reasonable in women after menopause, particularly if the premenopausal level was borderline. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A healthy 34-year-old asks for every heart blood test at her annual physical. Which test should not be ordered routinely?","choices":["A lipid profile","A natriuretic peptide","A blood pressure check","A glucose level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Routine natriuretic peptides in healthy patients are something not to order; they are for suspected heart failure and dyspnea triage. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 65-year-old had a stent placed yesterday and his chest pain has returned. Which use of troponin applies?","choices":["Guiding heart failure therapy","Screening for statin myopathy","Detecting reinfarction","Refining long-term risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Troponins detect reocclusion and reinfarction, and procedure-related infarction, among their applications beyond the first diagnosis. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 73-year-old with known heart failure has B-type natriuretic peptide checked at each visit. Which is a recognized use of serial natriuretic peptides?","choices":["Monitoring the course of disease","Detecting reinfarction after a stent","Diagnosing statin myopathy","Screening healthy adults yearly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beyond the rule-out, they help guide therapy, monitor the disease course and stratify risk, including cardiovascular mortality and likelihood of readmission. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 44-year-old with atypical chest pain has a measurable high-sensitivity troponin that is below the 99th percentile limit. What pitfall of the assay does this illustrate?","choices":["It cannot detect small infarctions","It cross-reacts with creatine kinase","It detects troponin in healthy people","It is falsely low with obesity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sensitivity is so high that measurable troponin turns up in healthy people, so the entire clinical picture has to be considered. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
